      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "# Callout Blocks\n",
        "In @Stock2020, key concepts are highlighted in bold and defined within the relevant text of each chapter, while the main ideas are summarized in Key Concept boxes. We adopt the same approach and use a Quarto callout block for the key concept boxes, as illustrated in the following example:\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.callout-note icon=false}\n",
        "## Key Concept 9.2: Variance and standard deviation\n",
        "Let $Y$ be a random variable with mean $\\mu_Y$. The variance of $Y$ is defined as:\n",
        "\n",
        "* Discrete case: $\\sigma^2_Y=\\E[(Y-\\mu_Y)^2]=\\sum_{i=1}^k(y_i-\\mu_Y)^2\\times P(Y=y_i)$, where $y_i$ are the possible values of $Y$.\n",
        "* Continuous case: $\\sigma^2_Y=\\E[(Y-\\mu_Y)^2]=\\int_D (y-\\mu_Y)^2f_Y(y)\\text{d}y$, where $D$ is the support of $Y$.\n",
        "\n",
        "The square root of the variance is called the standard deviation of $Y$ and is denoted by $\\sigma_Y$. The units of the standard deviation are the same as the units of $Y$.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "We also use a callout block to state econometric assumptions. For example, we use the following callout block for introducing the least squares assumptions:\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.callout-important icon=false title=\"Assumptions\"}\n",
        "1. The zero-conditional mean assumption: $\\E(u_i |X_i) = 0$, i.e., the conditional distribution of $u_i$ given $X_i$ has a mean of $0$. \n",
        "2. The random sampling assumption: $(X_i, Y_i):\\, i =1,2,\\dots,n$ are independently and identically distributed (i.i.d.) across observations.\n",
        "3. The no large outliers assumption: $\\E(X_i^4)<\\infty$ and $\\E(Y_i^4)<\\infty$.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "# Data for Applications\n",
        "For each methodological topic, @Stock2020 provide an application based on real-world data. We reproduce all tables, figures, and estimation results using the same datasets provided on the textbook's [web page](https://www.princeton.edu/~mwatson/Stock-Watson_4E/Stock-Watson-Resources-4e.html). The datasets are also available in the \"data\" folder of this book's GitHub repository.\n",
        "\n",
        "# Acknowledgements\n",
        "We use the theme from the book [R for Data Science (2e)](https://r4ds.hadley.nz/) as the foundation for producing this book. "
